  "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Smile_s_first_ultraviolet_footage_shows_auroral_substorm-1.jpeg?w=512&h=512&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint",
  "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 6 - A Complete Auroral Oval from SMILE</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-6-a-complete-auroral-oval-from-smile/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Have you ever seen a complete auroral oval? You can&#039;t see one from the ground because it makes too large a circle around one of Earth&#039;s magnetic poles. But spacecraft high above the Earth can see them. The featured video from ESA and CAS&#039;s robotic SMILE spacecraft shows not only a full auroral oval, but using ultraviolet light, one that occurred during the day. The time-lapse covers about an hour in late July and shows visually how variable and turbulent auroras really are. The points of light on the sides are distant stars that appear to move only because SMILE&#039;s camera view shifts as the spacecraft orbits the Earth. A goal of SMILE is to better understand how the Sun&#039;s wind interacts with the Earth&#039;s magnetosphere -- and so better understand how to protect astronauts, spacecraft, and ground-based electrical grids from solar storms.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: a spokey image\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"a complete auroral oval from smile\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"A Complete Auroral Oval from SMILE\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-6-a-complete-auroral-oval-from-smile/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Smile_s_first_ultraviolet_footage_shows_auroral_substorm-1.jpeg?w=512&#038;h=512&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Smile_s_first_ultraviolet_footage_shows_auroral_substorm-1.jpeg?w=512&#038;h=512&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A round Earth takes up most of the video. The dayside and nightside are visible. A full auroral oval is visible surrounding Earth&#039;s North Pole. There are some bright objects that speckle the background.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-06T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-06T10:18:42-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"A Complete Auroral Oval from SMILE\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Smile_s_first_ultraviolet_footage_shows_auroral_substorm-1.jpeg?w=512&#038;h=512&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 6<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2013/october/n891_block.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2013/october/n891_block.jpg\" alt=\"See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> A Complete Auroral Oval from SMILE </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.esa.int/\">ESA</a>, <a href=\"https://english.cas.cn/\">CAS</a>, <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Smile\">SMILE</a>, <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Smile/Smile_s_ultraviolet_vision\">UVI</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Have you ever seen a complete auroral oval? You can't see one from the ground because it makes too <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=ap970402.html\">large a circle</a> around one of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2004-september-19-earths-north-magnetic-pole/\">Earth's magnetic pole</a>s. But spacecraft high above <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/\">the Earth</a> can see them. The <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Images/2026/09/Smile_s_first_ultraviolet_footage_shows_auroral_substorm\">featured video</a> from <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/\">ESA</a> and <a href=\"https://english.cas.cn/\">CAS</a>'s robotic <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Smile/Smile_factsheet2\">SMILE spacecraft</a> shows not only a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-march-24-aurorae-and-lightning-on-jupiter/\">full auroral oval</a>, but using <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/10_ultravioletwaves/\">ultraviolet light</a>, one that occurred during the day. The time-lapse covers about an hour in late July and shows visually how <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-june-1-pulsating-aurora-over-iceland/\">variable</a> and turbulent <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/aurora/en/\">auroras</a> really are. The points of light on the sides are <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-april-6-earendel-a-star-in-the-early-universe/\">distant star</a>s that appear to move only because <a href=\"https://sci.esa.int/web/smile/-/59194-ultra-violet-imager\">SMILE's camera</a> view shifts as the spacecraft <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/catalog-of-earth-satellite-orbits/\">orbits</a> the Earth. A goal of SMILE is to <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/calico-kitten-standing-in-front-of-macbook-pro-g3B53PbBfwU\">better understand</a> how the Sun's wind interacts with the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/science-research/earth-science/earths-magnetosphere-protecting-our-planet-from-harmful-space-energy/\">Earth's magnetosphere</a> -- and so better understand how to protect astronauts, spacecraft, and ground-based <a href=\"https://www.spaceweather.gov/impacts/electric-power-transmission\">electrical grids</a> from <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/apod-2025-september-15-earth-during-a-powerful-solar-storm/\">solar storms</a>.<br><br>Tomorrow's picture: a spokey image\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-5-m104-the-sombrero-galaxys-tidal-streams/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>",
